   $q$The graph of $f(x) = \frac{2x^2+5x-3}{x^2+3x}$ has a hole. What are the coordinates of the hole?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$(-3, \\frac{7}{3})$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$(-3, -\\frac{7}{3})$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"sign-error-dividing-negatives"},{"text":"$(-3, 0)$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"assumed-hole-on-x-axis"},{"text":"$(3, \\frac{5}{3})$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-opposite-of-excluded-value"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$(-3, \frac{7}{3})$$q$, $q$Factor: $2x^2 + 5x - 3 = (2x - 1)(x + 3)$ and $x^2 + 3x = x(x + 3)$. The factor $(x + 3)$ cancels, leaving $\frac{2x - 1}{x}$ with a hole at $x = -3$. Its height is $\frac{2(-3) - 1}{-3} = \frac{-7}{-3} = \frac{7}{3}$, so the hole is $(-3, \frac{7}{3})$. The factor $x$ does not cancel, so $x = 0$ is a vertical asymptote, not a hole.$q$, 'draft', null)
