   $q$The graph of $f(x) = \frac{x^2+x}{x^2-4}$ crosses its horizontal asymptote at one point. At what $x$-value does this happen?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$x = 4$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"sign-error-solving"},{"text":"$x = -1$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"gave-x-intercept"},{"text":"$x = 2$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-vertical-asymptote"},{"text":"$x = -4$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$x = -4$$q$, $q$The degrees are equal, so the horizontal asymptote is $y = \frac{1}{1} = 1$. Set $f(x) = 1$: $x^2 + x = x^2 - 4$, so $x = -4$. Check: $f(-4) = \frac{16 - 4}{16 - 4} = 1$. The choice $x = -1$ is an $x$-intercept, where $f(x) = 0$, not where it equals $1$.$q$, 'draft', null)
