  ('ea88e4b9-3921-54ae-896e-542b3e4ff611', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'rational-functions'), null, 'hard',
   $q$What is the slant (oblique) asymptote of $f(x) = \frac{x^3-2x^2+x-1}{x^2+1}$?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$y = x + 2$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"sign-error-in-division"},{"text":"$y = x - 2$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$y = 1$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-leading-coefficient-ratio"},{"text":"$y = x$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"divided-leading-terms-only"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$y = x - 2$$q$, $q$Use long division. $x^3 \div x^2 = x$, and $x(x^2 + 1) = x^3 + x$; subtracting leaves $-2x^2 - 1$. Then $-2x^2 \div x^2 = -2$, and $-2(x^2 + 1) = -2x^2 - 2$; subtracting leaves $1$. So $f(x) = x - 2 + \frac{1}{x^2 + 1}$, and the slant asymptote is $y = x - 2$. The choice $y = x$ stops after the first step of the division.$q$, 'draft', null)
